    "name": "enterprise-ssh-mcp-server",
    "version": "0.3.4"
  },
  "protocolVersion": null,
  "instructions": "This server provides SSH/SFTP tools for configured remote hosts. For a new host that is not saved as a Profile, use ssh_connect for a temporary direct connection; host may be a hostname/IP with a separate username, or username@hostname. For password-authenticated Windows SSH hosts, prefer ssh_saved_credential_status and ssh_run_saved_credential with a locally enrolled DPAPI alias; never request passwords as MCP arguments. Enroll locally using Set-SshMcpCredential.ps1 and a host-key SHA256 hex verified independently. A host and port identify the destination but do not by themselves authenticate the user; use the authentication mechanism required by the server. For InstaBox SSH tokens, the ibssh_… token is the SSH username itself; it needs no password or private key. Treat this username as a bearer secret; responses redact it and it should not be saved in a shared .env file. The current ssh2 persistent-session backend cannot negotiate InstaBox's OpenSSH host-certificate algorithm. ssh_probe_host_key can inspect the certificate without authentication; ssh_connect refuses this target before sending the token. For a one-shot command, use ssh_run_openssh_temporary only after a host-specific CA fingerprint has been independently verified and pinned in the local MCP process environment variable SSH_MCP_TRUSTED_HOST_CA_FINGERPRINTS. Tool arguments cannot create or override this trust anchor. Call ssh_trust_host_ca only when the CA key matches that local pin and the user wants permanent known_hosts trust; prefer usernameEnvVar to avoid passing a token in tool arguments. Persistent Shell/SFTP/tunnel support for this certificate backend remains future work. InstaBox's SSH gateway does not support agent forwarding or remote forwarding. Never silently trust an unknown host key or CA fingerprint. For quick remote inspection or health checks, prefer ssh_check_profile. For one-shot commands on a configured profile, prefer ssh_run_profile instead of ssh_connect_profile + ssh_exec. Use ssh_connect_profile plus session tools only when the user explicitly needs a persistent session, shell, SFTP workflow, transfer job, or tunnel. Do not use local shell commands to simulate remote SSH checks when an SSH profile tool can do the work. Dangerous tools such as tunnels, rm, chmod, chown, and disconnect_all are hidden unless explicitly enabled by runtime config.",
  "durationMs": 11311,
  "stderr": ""
